    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost). The pork and the cheese are the two real costs here, and the beans, corn and tortillas that fill out the pan are close to free per serving.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy the block of cheddar and shred it.\u003c/strong\u003e This bake uses a lot of cheese, and block cheese runs cheaper per ounce than bagged shreds while melting noticeably better.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCorn tortillas over flour for a layered bake.\u003c/strong\u003e They cost less, they hold their structure under sauce, and they will not go gummy in the middle of the pan.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCook the pork shoulder in the slow cooker the day before.\u003c/strong\u003e Splitting the job across two days costs nothing and turns this into a 20 minute assembly on the night you actually want to eat it.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned black beans and corn round out the pan for pennies.\u003c/strong\u003e They add bulk, fiber and texture so you need less meat and less cheese per square."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Slow cook the pork shoulder with salt, black pepper, ground cumin and chili powder until it shreds, then pull it and drain off the excess liquid.",
                    "Cook the diced onion and chopped green bell peppers in the olive oil until soft, then add the minced garlic for the last minute.",
                    "Toss the pulled pork with the cooked vegetables, the barbecue sauce and about half the enchilada sauce until everything is coated.",
                    "Spread a thin layer of the remaining enchilada sauce in a large baking pan, then layer tortillas, pork mixture, drained black beans, drained corn and shredded cheddar. Repeat the layers until the pan is full.",
                    "Finish with tortillas, the last of the enchilada sauce and a heavy layer of cheddar on top.",
                    "Bake at 375F for 30 to 35 minutes until it bubbles at the edges, then let it rest 15 minutes before cutting so the layers set."
                ],
